<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio de Nebrija</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1444–1522</span> · Also known as Elio Antonio de Nebrija</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio de Nebrija was a Castilian humanist who taught at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> and Alcalá. His Gramática de la lengua castellana of 1492, presented to Isabella I, was the first printed grammar of a modern European vernacular. He also wrote a widely used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> grammar and dictionaries and worked on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complutensian-polyglot-bible">Complutensian Polyglot Bible</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-de-nebrija.jpg" alt="Antonio de Nebrija" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reproducción del grabado de Brieva publicado por la Calcografía nacional. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_de_Nebrija_(1892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
